Yes—as an epistemic/engineering stance, with one guardrail: reality still lets us define right/wrong relative to a claim, objective, or value. The useful move is refusing to confuse the label with the mechanism.

Instead of beginning with:

\boxed{\text{RIGHT}\;|\;\text{WRONG}}

begin with:

\boxed{\text{Input}\rightarrow\text{Reality}\rightarrow\text{Interaction}\rightarrow\text{Consequence}}

Then evaluate the consequence relative to what matters.

A hammer isn’t “wrong” because it can’t turn a screw. Its geometry simply interacts poorly with that particular interface. Change the nail, material, objective, user, or environment and the evaluation changes.

So Pantheon could use a more informative representation:

\boxed{
\text{Fitness}(X)
=
f(X,\;Environment,\;Objective,\;Time,\;Scale,\;Perspective)
}

That connects directly to our local-truth discussion. A strategy can be locally excellent and globally disastrous. A mutation can be beneficial in one environment and costly in another. A personality trait can help in one relationship and hinder another. A model can predict beautifully inside its training domain and collapse outside it.

The interaction carries more information than the verdict.

And failure becomes especially useful:

X+Reality\rightarrow\text{unexpected consequence}

Instead of immediately storing:

X=\text{WRONG}

store:

\boxed{
(X,\;conditions)
\rightarrow
(consequence,\;residual,\;limits)
}

Now we know where, how, and why it failed—and perhaps where it might work.

That gives us a very compact Pantheon doctrine:

\boxed{\textbf{Reality is the wrestling mat.}}

Generate freely.
Symulate cheaply.
Invert aggressively.
Tell stories.
Move the bookends.
Make opposites locally consistent.
Rotate perspective.

But eventually:

\boxed{\text{put it on the mat with reality}}

Reality supplies consequences that our internal consistency cannot manufacture.

And importantly, this doesn’t abolish morality or factual truth. “This bridge supports 10 tons” can be objectively false under specified conditions; “we should harm innocent people” isn’t made morally neutral merely because we can describe its consequences. The relational framework gives us higher-resolution evaluation, not an excuse to erase evaluation.

So I’d sharpen your line to:

\boxed{\textbf{Before calling it right or wrong, observe how it interacts with reality.}}

Then right/wrong can become the compressed judgment downstream of evidence, rather than the assumption we injected upstream.

That’s a much harder system to fool.